            "The <strong>surgical hand scrub</strong> removes transient organisms and reduces the resident flora of the hands and forearms for the length of an operation. Before the first scrub of the day the nurse removes jewellery, checks her nails are short and clean, puts on cap, mask and eye protection, adjusts the water and does a general pre-wash. The scrub itself — with <strong>4 % chlorhexidine gluconate</strong> or <strong>7.5 % povidone-iodine</strong>, or an alcohol-based surgical hand rub applied by its own protocol after an initial wash — lasts <strong>three to five minutes</strong> and works systematically: nails cleaned with a pick under running water on the first scrub of the day, then each finger, the webs, the palm and the back of each hand, each side of the forearm to two inches above the elbow, keeping the <strong>hands above the elbows</strong> at all times so that water runs from the clean fingertips to the dirtier elbows and never back. The hands are rinsed from fingertips to elbows, the taps are closed with the elbow or a foot control, and the nurse walks to the operating room with her hands held up and away from her body, dries each hand and arm with a separate half of a <strong>sterile towel</strong> from fingers to elbow, and <strong>gowns</strong> — lifting the gown by the inside only, letting it fall open, pushing her arms into the sleeves without her hands emerging, and having the circulating nurse tie it from behind — and <strong>gloves</strong> by the closed method, in which the hands never leave the cuffs until they are inside the gloves. Once gowned, the <strong>sterile area of the gown</strong> is the front from the chest to the level of the sterile field and the sleeves from two inches above the elbow to the cuff; the back, the neck, the axillae and everything below table level are not sterile, and the hands are kept above the waist, in front and in sight.",
