            "Three words are used loosely on the wards and precisely in theatre. <strong>Cleaning</strong> removes visible soil and most organisms with detergent, water and friction, and it <strong>always comes first</strong>, because blood, pus and tissue shield organisms from disinfectants and from steam and inactivate chlorine — you cannot sterilise a dirty instrument. <strong>Disinfection</strong> kills most organisms but <strong>not bacterial spores</strong>: chlorine solutions, alcohol, boiling, and chemical disinfectants such as glutaraldehyde for a short immersion. <strong>Sterilisation</strong> destroys <strong>all</strong> microbial life, spores included. Which level an item needs follows the <strong>Spaulding classification</strong>: <strong>critical</strong> items that enter sterile tissue or the bloodstream — surgical instruments, implants, needles, catheters that enter the bladder — must be <strong>sterile</strong>; <strong>semi-critical</strong> items that touch mucous membranes or broken skin — laryngoscope blades, endoscopes, vaginal specula, anaesthetic breathing circuits — need <strong>high-level disinfection</strong> at least and sterilisation where possible; <strong>non-critical</strong> items that touch intact skin — blood pressure cuffs, trolleys, pulse oximeter probes, the operating table — need <strong>cleaning and low-level disinfection</strong>.",
            "Used instruments follow a one-way path through the sterile services department: <strong>decontamination</strong> at the point of use (in many Ghanaian facilities a <strong>ten-minute soak in 0.5 % chlorine</strong> immediately after use, in gloves and apron, to make the instruments safer to handle), <strong>cleaning</strong> with detergent and brushes under the surface of the water to avoid splashing, with hinged instruments open and lumens flushed, rinsing and drying, <strong>inspection</strong> and function testing, <strong>packaging</strong> in wraps, pouches or containers with an internal indicator, <strong>sterilisation</strong>, cooling, and <strong>storage</strong> in closed, dry cupboards away from the floor and the sink. The <strong>steam autoclave</strong> is the workhorse: steam under pressure reaches temperatures above boiling, and it is the <strong>heat</strong> delivered by the steam, not the pressure itself, that kills — <strong>121 °C at about 15 psi for 15–20 minutes</strong>, or <strong>134 °C at about 30 psi for 3–4 minutes</strong>, with longer cycles for wrapped and dense loads. Steam must touch every surface, so the chamber is loaded loosely, packs are placed on edge, containers are open and hollow items are tilted to drain. <strong>Dry heat</strong> (160 °C for two hours or 170 °C for one hour) is used for glass, powders and oils that steam cannot penetrate. <strong>Chemical sterilisation</strong> — ethylene oxide gas, hydrogen peroxide plasma, peracetic acid, or immersion in 2 % glutaraldehyde for ten hours — is for heat-sensitive items such as flexible endoscopes; glutaraldehyde for 20–45 minutes achieves only high-level disinfection and is toxic to staff. <strong>Boiling</strong> for twenty minutes disinfects but does <strong>not sterilise</strong>, and is a last resort for non-critical and semi-critical items where there is no autoclave.",
