            "A trolley carries fifty names but only five jobs, and the student who learns the jobs recognises the names. <strong>Cutting and dissecting</strong>: the scalpel (handles 3 and 4; blades 10 and 20 for skin, 11 for stab incisions, 15 for fine work), <strong>Mayo</strong> scissors for tough tissue and sutures, <strong>Metzenbaum</strong> scissors for delicate dissection — never used to cut sutures. <strong>Grasping and holding</strong>: dissecting forceps — <strong>toothed</strong> for skin and fascia, <strong>non-toothed (plain)</strong> for bowel, vessels and delicate tissue that teeth would tear — <strong>Allis</strong> and <strong>Babcock</strong> tissue forceps (Babcock's rounded jaws hold bowel without crushing it), and <strong>sponge-holding forceps</strong> for skin preparation and swabbing deep cavities. <strong>Clamping and occluding</strong>: <strong>artery forceps</strong> — mosquito, Spencer Wells, Kelly, and the toothed <strong>Kocher</strong> — the commonest instrument on every tray, stopping bleeding by crushing the vessel until it is tied or coagulated; crushing and non-crushing <strong>bowel clamps</strong>; and towel clips. <strong>Retracting and exposing</strong>: handheld retractors — Langenbeck, Czerny, Deaver, Morris — held by an assistant who must not lean on the patient or drift, and <strong>self-retaining</strong> retractors — Balfour for the abdomen, Weitlaner and Gelpi for smaller wounds — that hold themselves open. <strong>Suturing</strong>: the <strong>needle holder</strong> (Mayo-Hegar), whose short cross-hatched jaws grip a needle and never touch tissue. Add <strong>suction</strong> (Yankauer for the field, Poole for the abdomen) and the <strong>diathermy</strong> and the trolley is complete.",
            "<strong>Sutures</strong> are either <strong>absorbable</strong> — catgut (plain and chromic, from animal collagen), polyglactin (Vicryl), polyglycolic acid, polydioxanone (PDS, slow) — used for tissue that heals and does not need permanent support (bowel, bladder, subcutaneous layers, the uterus) and for skin in children, or <strong>non-absorbable</strong> — silk, nylon, polypropylene (Prolene), polyester and stainless steel wire — used for skin that will have its sutures removed, for vessel anastomoses, mesh and tendons. <strong>Size</strong> runs from the heavy 2 and 1 through 0 to the fine 2-0, 3-0, 4-0 and beyond — the more zeros, the finer the thread. Needles are <strong>cutting</strong> (triangular point) for skin and tough tissue and <strong>round-bodied</strong> (taper point) for bowel, vessels and anything that must not be cut. <strong>Passing instruments</strong> is a skill: the instrument is placed firmly into the surgeon's waiting palm in the position of use, ring handles first, so that she does not have to look away from the wound; <strong>sharps</strong> — scalpels, loaded needle holders — are passed in a <strong>kidney dish or a neutral zone</strong>, never hand to hand, and announced; blades are mounted and removed with a needle holder or blade remover, never fingers.",
            "The <strong>surgical count</strong> is the scrub and circulating nurse's absolute responsibility, not a formality for the record. <strong>Swabs</strong> (X-ray-detectable, in bundles of five), <strong>needles</strong>, <strong>blades</strong>, <strong>instruments</strong> and small items (bulldog clamps, vessel loops, clip cartridges) are counted <strong>aloud, together, by two people</strong> — the scrub nurse and the circulating nurse — each item seen and separated: <strong>before the operation begins</strong> (the baseline, with every bundle broken open and counted — a bundle of four is removed from the room, never made up), <strong>whenever items are added</strong> during the operation (recorded on the count board at once), <strong>whenever staff change</strong> over, <strong>before a cavity or an organ is closed</strong> (the first closing count), and <strong>at skin closure</strong> (the final count). Swabs used in the wound stay in the room, hung on a rack in fives, until the final count is correct; swabs are never cut or taken out of the room on a trolley or in a bin; small swabs are never used in an open cavity unless mounted on a holder. If the count is <strong>wrong</strong>, the surgeon is told <strong>immediately</strong> and before the wound is closed, the count is repeated, the drapes, the floor, the bins and the field are searched, the wound is explored, and if the item is still missing an <strong>X-ray</strong> is taken before the patient leaves the theatre; the discrepancy, the actions and the outcome are recorded and an incident report is written. A <strong>retained swab or instrument</strong> is a never event: it causes abscess, obstruction, a second operation and sometimes death, and it is always traceable to a count that was skipped, rushed or done by one person.",
