            "A <strong>specimen</strong> is often the whole point of the operation, and a lost or mislabelled one means a lost diagnosis, a wrong treatment or a second anaesthetic. The scrub nurse receives it from the surgeon with its name and site stated aloud, the circulating nurse places it in the <strong>correct container</strong> — <strong>10 % formalin</strong> for histology, a <strong>dry sterile container</strong> for microbiology and culture, <strong>fresh in saline and sent at once</strong> for frozen section — and <strong>labels it in the theatre, at the time</strong>, with the patient's name, hospital number, date, the exact site and side, the surgeon and the test required, completes the request form and enters it in the specimen register; specimens are read back at sign out and dispatched by a named person. Amputated limbs, placentas and fetal remains are handled with the respect and the documentation the policy requires, and a family's request for a limb for burial is answered, not ignored.",
            "<strong>Sharps</strong> injure theatre staff more than anyone else. The neutral zone, announced passing, blade removers, one-handed technique, no recapping, a sharps container at the table and the count all reduce injuries; after a needle-stick the wound is bled gently and washed under running water, the injury is reported at once, the source patient's hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV status are sought with consent, and <strong>post-exposure prophylaxis</strong> for HIV is started as soon as possible — within hours, and within 72 at the latest — alongside hepatitis B immunoglobulin or vaccine if the nurse is not immune. <strong>Diathermy (electrosurgery)</strong> cuts and coagulates with high-frequency current. Monopolar diathermy needs a <strong>return plate</strong>, placed by the circulating nurse on a clean, dry, well-perfused, muscular area — the thigh or the buttock — as near the operation as practical, away from bony prominences, scars, hairy skin (clipped if necessary), metal implants and pacemakers, in full contact and checked; burns happen at a plate with poor contact, under <strong>pooled alcohol-based skin preparation</strong> that was not allowed to dry, where the patient's skin touches metal, and at jewellery. Patients with pacemakers need bipolar diathermy or a plan with the cardiologist; the plate site is inspected and documented at the end. A <strong>tourniquet</strong> on a limb has its pressure and its time of inflation recorded and announced, and is discussed further in lesson 2.2.",
            "<strong>Latex allergy</strong> can cause fatal anaphylaxis under anaesthesia, when it is hard to recognise. The patients at risk — those with spina bifida or many previous operations, health workers who wear gloves daily, and people allergic to banana, avocado, kiwi or chestnut — are identified in the preoperative assessment, placed <strong>first on the list</strong> in a room cleared of latex overnight, nursed with latex-free gloves, catheters, drains, tourniquets and syringes, labelled on the band, the notes and the door, and have adrenaline ready. <strong>Theatre waste</strong> follows the national colour code: <strong>black</strong> for general waste, <strong>yellow</strong> for infectious waste including blood-soaked swabs, drapes, gloves and anatomical waste, and sharps in a rigid yellow safety box sealed at three-quarters full, and <strong>brown</strong> for pharmaceutical and chemical waste; segregation is at the point of generation by the person who made the waste, linen is bagged in the room without shaking, spills are covered with chlorine after the organic matter is removed, and the room is cleaned between cases and terminally at the end of the list.",
