          alt: "Choosing an intravenous fluid — what has the patient lost, and where do you want the fluid to go? Isotonic (normal saline 0.9 %, Ringer's lactate) stays in the vessels — use for shock, bleeding, severe dehydration and burns. Hypotonic (half-strength saline, 5 % dextrose once used) moves into the cells — never in head injury, it worsens brain swelling. Hypertonic (3 % saline, 50 % dextrose) pulls water out of cells — specialist use. Why 5 % dextrose is not a resuscitation fluid: the cells consume the sugar and what remains is plain water spread through every compartment. Signs of too much: breathlessness, crackles, full neck veins, rising weight. The most honest measures: daily weight, a truthful intake and output chart, hourly urine output.",
